      "name": "Semi-Private Greenwich Village Walking Tour",
      "description": "<div><p>Begin your two-hour exploration at the corner of 6th Avenue and Waverly Place, outside the Waverly Diner, where your guide introduces you to Greenwich Village’s storied past. Stroll over to Washington Square Park, once a gathering place for artists and protestors alike, and visit highlights like the former home of Edgar Allan Poe and the historic Washington Square Hotel.</p><p>Continue to the Brown Building on New York University’s campus, the site of the 1911 Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire—a pivotal moment in the American labor movement that ultimately led to stronger workers’ rights. From there, meander down McDougal Street, hearing how iconic performers like Bob Dylan, Jimi Hendrix, and Bruce Springsteen got their start in local cafés and music clubs. Pass by the Comedy Cellar, where top comedians such as Jimmy Fallon, Jerry Seinfeld, and Dave Chappelle still appear.</p><p>Finally, pause for a tip on the neighborhood’s best pizza slice before winding down Christopher Street, home to The Stonewall Inn. Learn how this landmark bar became a catalyst for the gay rights movement and take in its enduring legacy, concluding your tour with a richer appreciation for Greenwich Village’s diverse cultural tapestry.</p></div>",
