      "name": "Harlem Walking Tour: Mount Morris Park Historic District w/ Lunch",
      "description": "<div><p>In 1971, the New York City Landmarks Preservation Commission acknowledged the cultural and architectural importance of the Mount Morris Park Historic District. On this immersive tour, see firsthand the unaltered streetscapes of brownstone residences and churches that capture Harlem’s late 19th- and early 20th-century elegance. Stroll along 125th Street—often called “the heartbeat of Harlem”—to discover landmarks like the Hotel Teresa, St. Martin’s Episcopal Church, and the Apollo Theater. Visit a local park renowned for hosting the 1969 “Black Woodstock” and view the city’s last standing fire watchtower. Explore enclaves once home to Tammany Hall bosses, and learn about the neighborhood’s historic “Doctor’s Row.” Each stop offers a glimpse into Harlem’s vibrant past—and its ongoing legacy as a hub of culture, resilience, and community.</p></div>",
